EXT. LIFEBOAT ADRIFT, SOUTHERN OCEAN - NOON

A grey swell. A hull the colour of old teeth. The sun a dull coin behind cloud.

Three figures crowd the boat. CAPTAIN ISOLDE VANDERHOEK-MWAMBA (50s, cracked lips, officer's cap tied under her chin) sits at the tiller, jaw tight.

Across from her, ENSIGN REMY LAFAYETTE-OSUNDE (20s, one arm in a makeshift sling, blistered nose) holds a half-empty water tin in his good hand.

In the bow, the ship's cook, MARGUERITE "MAGS" ODELL-PRZYBYLSKI (60s, apron still on, salt in her eyebrows), stares at the horizon with her chin on her knees.

REMY
Captain, the ration.

ISOLDE
Two sips. Each.

REMY
Last night it was three.

ISOLDE
Last night I thought we'd be rescued by today.

MAGS
(not turning)
That's what I thought yesterday.

REMY
Mags, don't.

MAGS
I'm only saying.

ISOLDE
Pass the tin.

Remy hands it over. She takes a sip, wipes her mouth, passes it on. Mags takes hers without looking.

MAGS
Captain, how long do you give us?

ISOLDE
I don't give things.

MAGS
How long do you think?

ISOLDE
Three days. Four if the rain comes.

REMY
And if it doesn't?

ISOLDE
Then we'll know.

Silence. The swell lifts them, lowers them.

MAGS
There.

REMY
Where?

MAGS
There! A light!

Remy scrambles up, rocking the boat. Isolde grabs the gunwale. A speck of white glints on the horizon.

REMY
Ship. It's a ship.

ISOLDE
It's a glare.

REMY
It's moving!

MAGS
It's moving!

ISOLDE
(quietly)
It's a gull.

The speck rises, banks, and becomes a seabird, wheeling above the waves. It cries once and flies away from them.

Remy sinks back. Mags laughs, a cracked little sound. Then she cries, then she laughs again.

ISOLDE
Mags.

MAGS
I know.

ISOLDE
Pass the tin.

MAGS
It's empty.

ISOLDE
Pass it anyway.
